          <p class="text-sm font-medium text-indigo-600 uppercase tracking-wide mb-3">FAQ</p>
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-gray-900 sm:text-4xl">
            Questions vSphere administrators ask
          </h2>
        </div>

        <dl class="divide-y divide-gray-200">
          <div class="py-8">
            <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Does it work with a standalone ESXi host that has no vCenter?</dt>
            <dd class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Yes. The vcenter receiver speaks the vSphere SDK, which a standalone ESXi host exposes too. Point <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">VCENTER_ENDPOINT</code> at the host, create a Read-Only user in the ESXi Host Client, and the host registers as its own "vCenter" in OneUptime with its VMs, datastores, and resource pools underneath. Clusters, datacenter-level health, and vSAN counters need a real vCenter, since standalone hosts do not have them.</dd>
          </div>

          <div class="py-8">
            <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Which vSphere versions and editions are supported?</dt>
            <dd class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">vCenter Server and ESXi 7.0 and later, including vSphere 8. The agent only uses the standard vSphere API that every edition exposes, so it works the same whether you run vSphere Standard, Enterprise Plus, VMware Cloud Foundation, a VMware vSphere Foundation subscription, or a perpetual licence you are still on. OneUptime is not a VMware or Broadcom product and does not depend on any VMware licence tier, add-on, or content pack.</dd>
          </div>

          <div class="py-8">
            <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Do I need to install anything on ESXi or inside my VMs?</dt>
            <dd class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">No. The agent is a single OpenTelemetry Collector container that runs on any Docker host with a route to vCenter on TCP 443. It polls vCenter over HTTPS with a read-only account and never touches ESXi or your guests. The <a href="/product/host" class="text-indigo-600 hover:text-indigo-700 font-medium">host agent</a> is an optional extra for VMs where you also want OS-level processes, logs, and traces.</dd>
          </div>

          <div class="py-8">
            <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">What permissions does the agent need?</dt>
            <dd class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">The built-in <strong class="font-medium text-gray-800">Read-Only</strong> role on the top-level vCenter object, with <em>Propagate to children</em> ticked. It reads inventory, performance counters, and vSAN statistics and nothing else. It cannot power a VM on or off, migrate anything, change a setting, or read a guest's disk. Credentials live in a <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">chmod 600</code> env file on the agent host and are never sent to OneUptime.</dd>
          </div>

          <div class="py-8">
            <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">How does this compare to Aria Operations (vRealize Operations)?</dt>
            <dd class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Aria Operations is a deep, vSphere-only analytics suite licensed per CPU or as part of a bundle. OneUptime covers the operational core — inventory, host and VM saturation, CPU ready, ballooning and swapping, datastore capacity, cluster and datacenter health, vSAN latency — and puts it beside the rest of your estate: Kubernetes, Docker, bare-metal hosts, network devices, services, and logs, with on-call, status pages, and incidents in the same product. If you want one place to be paged from rather than a second console, that is the difference.</dd>
          </div>

          <div class="py-8">
            <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Can I ship ESXi and vCenter logs too?</dt>
            <dd class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Yes. The agent's collector config includes a commented-out syslog listener. Enable it, point <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">Syslog.global.logHost</code> on each ESXi host at the agent, and the logs land on the vCenter's Logs tab, stamped with the same vCenter identity as the metrics.</dd>
          </div>

          <div class="py-8">
            <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">We are also evaluating Proxmox. Can we watch both?</dt>
            <dd class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Yes. OneUptime ships a separate <a href="/product/proxmox" class="text-indigo-600 hover:text-indigo-700 font-medium">Proxmox VE product</a> with the same shape — one agent per cluster, inventory, alert templates, dashboards. Run both side by side in one project during a migration and keep the same on-call rotation, status pages, and incident process across the two hypervisors.</dd>
          </div>

          <div class="py-8">
            <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Where do I start?</dt>
            <dd class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Create a project, open <strong class="font-medium text-gray-800">VMware</strong> in the sidebar, and follow the install card — it interpolates your ingestion key into the command for you. The <a href="/docs/telemetry/vmware" class="text-indigo-600 hover:text-indigo-700 font-medium">VMware agent docs</a> cover the read-only user, environment variables, what is collected, syslog, and troubleshooting; the <a href="/docs/monitor/vmware-monitor" class="text-indigo-600 hover:text-indigo-700 font-medium">VMware monitor docs</a> list every metric and template.</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
